-- title:
--   The trial segment $x_k + t(s\,d_k)$ lies in $\bar{\mathcal{L}}$ for $0 \le s \le \mu$, $0 \le t \le 1$ (p. 1050)
-- statement:
--   Let $f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ and let $x_k$, $d_k$ be the iterates and directions of a run, with $\mathcal{L} = \{y : f(y) \le f(x_0)\}$ and $\bar{\mathcal{L}} = \{x : \operatorname{dist}(x, \mathcal{L}) \le \mu d_{\max}\}$.
--
--   **Claim.** If $x_k \in \mathcal{L}$, $0 \le s \le \mu$ and $0 \le t \le 1$, then $x_k + t(s d_k) \in \bar{\mathcal{L}}$.
--
--   Indeed $x_k$ is at distance $0$ from $\mathcal{L}$, so $\operatorname{dist}(x_k + t(s d_k), \mathcal{L}) \le \|t(s d_k)\| = ts\,\|d_k\| \le \mu d_{\max}$. This is the geometric fact that makes $\bar{\mathcal{L}}$ the region on which the proof of Theorem 3.1 may take a Lipschitz constant for $\nabla f$: every line-search trial point generated by a step of length at most $\mu$ along a direction stays in $\bar{\mathcal{L}}$. The formalization computes distances in $[0,\infty]$, so the bound is monotone in the $(0,\infty]$-valued supremum $d_{\max}$, and the estimate is uniform in the step index $k$.
-- source:
--   Zhang, Hager, A Nonmonotone Line Search Technique and Its Application to Unconstrained Optimization, SIAM J. Optim. 14 (2004), p. 1050 (definition of L-bar as the mu*d_max neighbourhood of L, used throughout the proof of Theorem 3.1)

